  "//maxPerNflTeamByPos": "How many players from the same NFL team may be held at a position. RB 1 = never two backs out of one backfield: they split the touches that make either worth owning, and the injury that hands one the job removes the other (Andrew, Aug 2026, after the 2026-08-17 mock came out holding Irving+Gainwell and Pollard+Spears). Deliberately RB only -- two receivers on one offence are a much weaker correlation, and that same mock's Godwin-with-Irving drew no objection. Softer than a position cap: if it is the only thing standing between the engine and no legal pick it is given up first, and announced.",
  "maxPerNflTeamByPos": {
   "RB": 1
  },
  "//minCountByRound": "At least `count` players at this position by the END of `byRound` -- that round is INCLUDED. RB 3 by round 5 = three running backs inside the first five rounds (Andrew, Aug 2026; tightened from round 6 after the 2026-08-27 mock, where a round-6 deadline let a fourth receiver go at pick 44). A WR 2-by-round-5 quota was tried on 2026-08-31 and removed on 2026-09-02: together the two claimed all five window picks from slot 5, and the quotas bind on their own arithmetic with no joint check, so a single non-RB/WR pick early would have left one unpayable without warning. TE 1 by round 10 added 2026-09-02: safe beside the RB quota because their windows overlap loosely -- four players owed against ten picks -- so neither can starve the other. QB 1 by round 12 added 2026-09-03: with the QB floor at 12 this can only bind at the round-12 pick (140), where the debt of one meets the one pick left in its window -- so it means exactly 'a quarterback in round 12', and cannot collide with the other quotas, whose windows have closed. The inverse of minRoundByPos: a floor is a prohibition, which can always be met by taking somebody else, while this is an obligation that eventually has to be paid. It binds JUST IN TIME -- while enough of your picks remain inside the window to settle it later the board decides as normal, and only when the players still owed equal the picks left in the window does the candidate set narrow. From slot 5 the window is picks 5, 20, 29, 44 and 53, so the earliest it can bind is pick 29, and it never binds at all if you take backs early. If it can no longer be met -- more owed than picks left -- it stands down and says so rather than blocking the draft, because unlike a floor it is a claim about picks already spent. It is the LAST rule the relaxation ladder gives up.",
